nk would  explain why they were running around  almost butt-naked an animal first  screaming oh maybe that was just a  regular Friday night for a Viking  most of us have heard of the witch hunts  that took place in the 16th century but  the less well known werewolf hunts took  place in the same period interrogations  of werewolves often involve peeling back  a person's skin to see what was  underneath as it was a common belief  that werewolves turned their skin  inside-out on in human form the 16th  century saw a rise of people claiming to  be werewolves when committing murders  and other heinous crimes  in 1589 in the sleepy town of bed burg  just outside of Cologne Germany a man  named Peter Stumpf was executed for  cannibalism responsible for over a dozen  murders Stumpf claimed that he was in  possession of a belt that allowed him to  turn into a wolf so how does one become  a werewolf well he 4-game by the  earliest literature then people were  turned into werewolves as a form of  punishment if you behave like a beast  then the God would use lycanthropy to  turn you into one when lycanthropy was  used as a punishment the transformation  was almost always permanent though there  are stories where the offender was  turned into a wolf for a number of years  usually 7 or 9 and once they had done  their time they turned back into a human  and the curse was lifted in Norse and  Germanic folklore becoming a werewolf is  not seen as a punishment it's more of a  gift granting you increased strength and  speed these stories describe a belt made  from the skin of a wolf that allows the  wearer to become one in these depictions  the transformation is voluntary and not  determined by the phase of the moon but  if the belt is destroyed the ability to  transform disappears  there are other folktales were becoming  a werewolf requires the removal of one's  clothing they can only return to the  human form once they put on the clothes  they were originally wearing a French  tale written in the 12th century  features a baron named bisque Lavery who  vanishes every week for several days no  one including his wife knows where he  goes he eventually tells his wife he's  secret but he is a well and that his  clothing is the secret to his  transformation terrified of what she's  just heard his wife consults with a  knight who she happens to be having an  affair with and the two decide to steal  the Barons clothes unable to transform  back into his human form the Baron does  not return his wife marries the knight  and together they live long happy lives  is probably what I would say if this  tale did not contain werewolves the  Baron eventually returns in his werewolf  form and upon seeing his wife he bites  her nose off she is later banished along  with the knight and their children a  curse to be born without noses these  depictions of werewolves are personally  my least favorite their reliance on  their clothes makes them feel weak and  vulnerable in modern depictions  lycanthropy is most commonly shared  through the bite of scratch of an  existing werewolf there is an endless  list of ways that one can become a  werewolf but a rather common trope that  is carried throughout their ages is that  committing an unspeakable act will curse  one with lycanthropy  the taking of an innocent life being the  most common there are also those who are  unlucky enough to be born a werewolf if  we take a look at terry pratchett's book  series Discworld we can see that  werewolves are shown to be their own  race much like goblins and Dewar's  how was I to know the city watches  finest sergeant was aware the full moon  being required for a werewolf to  transform is something that we see a lot  less nowadays as it's become pretty  common for werewolves to transform when  and where they please the transformation  process itself has changed drastically  throughout the years from not being  mentioned much in early literature to  the spectacle it's now become in modern  film and television the transformation  appears in great detail and is almost  always depicted as being extremely  painful we see bones elongates and  change shape skin ruptures and tears and  the transformation can last for several  minutes and sometimes you just grow some  facial hair got a loved Teen Wolf right  the results of the transformation have  varied throughout the years with the  more traditional half-man half-wolf  appearance being replaced by the  enormous monsters that we have become  accustomed to see in today but why  exactly are the movies choosing to  dismiss the human element to the  werewolf in favor of the savage beast I  think for the most part this just boils  down to the fact that they can because  of the evolution of special effects it's  much easier to show a gruesome and  convincing transformation and through  CGI the werewolf can be shown as this  large terrifying monster sometimes  you're left of a confusing mix of both  monster and man  [Music]  maintaining some of their human features  when they transform makes it much easier  for us as the audience to be able to  relate to the werewolf as we can see the  conflict between their humanity and the  primal instinct of the wolf we often see  werewolves who struggle to accept or  even control their lycanthropy and it's  because of this redeeming feature that  the audience feels compassion towards  them on the other hand you have  franchises like underworld where it's an  all-out war between werewolves and  vampires and you have these huge hulking  alpha wolves which we have no attachment  to whatsoever but it works perfectly  fine in their universe so we've  established that the appearance of the  werewolf depends on context and despite  which one you may prefer you can argue  that they all have their place except  these ones so how does one end the  werewolf curse the simplest way to end  the curse is to die the only downside to  this option of course is that you're  dead which kind of sucks I guess there  are those who believe that a potion made  from Wolfsbane can kill lycanthropy  but Wolfsbane is also poisonous to  werewolves so take too much and you're  dead which we've really established as  bad calling a werewolf by their birth  name while they're a wolf can't cure  them this is personally my least  favorite of all the cures as it just  seems way too easy much more believable  cure for someone who's been turned is to  kill the werewolf that turned them there  are also many people who just believe  there is no cure  so this leads us into the methods of  killing a werewolf surprisingly enough  things that were killing human will also  kill a werewolf the problem lies we've  been getting close enough to do so  werewolves able to regenerate so if you  do not kill them quickly it is unlikely  that you will kill them at all it is  widely believed that werewolves have a  strong aversion towards silver and the  silver bullet to the heart or removing  their head of a silver blade or kill  them we've already mentioned that  Wolfsbane is like poison to a werewolf  this serves as a good way to slow down  and immobilize a werewolf and in large  amounts or inject it directly into their  heart  Wolfsbane can kill a werewolf the  easiest way to kill a werewolf is to  find them when they're in a human form  as this is when they are at their most  vulnerable however we've where was being  able to transform whenever they please  this may not be the case anymore when a  werewolf does eventually die their  corpse will often return to their human  form so we've arrived at our final  question why are we're also popular sex  appeal yep I said it werewolves are  strong assertive rugged alpha male type  characters who we find attractive and  most men aspire to be a little bit more  like  it's not just mayor werewolves though  films like Ginger Snaps and blood and  chocolate feature female werewolf  characters who display the same  attractive qualities in modern-day  stories werewolves and vampires and  natural enemies the reasons for their  hatred vary but it does guarantee an  epic conflict between two of the night's  most deadly predators so those of you  who dislike vampires will probably like  werewolves and of course some people  just like big scary monsters myself  included  like every other popular monster the  werewolf represents more than just a  vicious beast that scares us where we  choose to suppress our darkest desires  the werewolf either embraces them or  struggles to fight them the concept that  we can relate to  they represent the hidden evil in a  person the idea that everyone has an  inner beast waitin to be unleashed if  you've enjoyed this video then please  consider leaving a thumbs up as it helps  promote the video and allows the channel  to grow if there's anything you'd like  to discuss or have any ideas for future  videos then please let me know in the  comment section as always I've been your  host mythology and folklore explained
 

